[[:db/retract 17592186150167 :answer-field/choices 17592186150168] [:db/retract 17592186150167 :answer-field/choices 17592186150172] [:db/retract 17592186308124 :answer-field/choices 17592186307251] [:db/retract 17592186307992 :answer-field/choices 17592186305321] {:db/id 17592186152277 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c75ba2f7-e873-5513-857a-f8ee74ebb547" "question-47c9e11d-3763-56a1-a6fd-21b1255f236b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152278 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-16b925fc-3e3d-5f10-942e-39dcf0cbf5aa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151905 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n![](images/63/63e7eee4a168f339b9dfb1fb69ca67a3747d567020ade08c2bbab3121b530b72.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3b78d46d-4b29-5944-9afd-75dc247769fb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151906 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n![](images/57/573b2182d03d7e15927368117c3e610cc018dbcda0711754083a2d8a3c95320d.svg)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-46d6b627-57fc-5c4f-9c8a-dc31a7241e7a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c75ba2f7-e873-5513-857a-f8ee74ebb547" :question/id #uuid "c75ba2f7-e873-5513-857a-f8ee74ebb547" :question/math-academy-id "q-334026" :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${{e}^{x},\\,x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nSuppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{k}_{2}\\cdot x=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${k}_{1}$ and ${k}_{2}$ are constants.\n\nFor example:\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=0,$ we get  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get  \n{{field-2}}.\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is {{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}{e}^{x}+{k}_{2}x=0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(0)=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\n- If $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=0\\,⇒\\,2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=0.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{independent}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-01275dc9-5156-5923-9402-205cfa0d2994" "answer-field-8bdf06db-d344-5a02-bd9e-aa29815929d7" "answer-field-c1bf545f-c2ab-544f-8c0d-297f8adf4142" "answer-field-c28908a1-f4e3-564f-a061-6af102ecf123" "answer-field-1f3a14a1-7676-561a-9298-96af99e9a3d3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-01275dc9-5156-5923-9402-205cfa0d2994" :answer-field/id #uuid "01275dc9-5156-5923-9402-205cfa0d2994" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-e36b9598-f179-5762-9811-35f0a0c1da39" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10ba94b5-d5f6-561b-b2e2-a78ef02bf1f4" "answer-b048ca70-d1f0-56c3-a28c-d0e5a258697c" "answer-e36b9598-f179-5762-9811-35f0a0c1da39" "answer-b1b7e62e-b69d-5648-8b53-98d08560d756"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10ba94b5-d5f6-561b-b2e2-a78ef02bf1f4" :answer/id #uuid "10ba94b5-d5f6-561b-b2e2-a78ef02bf1f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{k}_{1}+{k}_{2}=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b048ca70-d1f0-56c3-a28c-d0e5a258697c" :answer/id #uuid "b048ca70-d1f0-56c3-a28c-d0e5a258697c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{k}_{1}-{k}_{2}=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e36b