hoices ["answer-44448b71-836e-5481-a958-fbda8470d5b7" "answer-e71ff228-1aa6-58e3-ac69-ec58903aae39"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44448b71-836e-5481-a958-fbda8470d5b7" :answer/id #uuid "44448b71-836e-5481-a958-fbda8470d5b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "all" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e71ff228-1aa6-58e3-ac69-ec58903aae39" :answer/id #uuid "e71ff228-1aa6-58e3-ac69-ec58903aae39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not all" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bfcf71a-fd64-5c92-96e0-a4da19acec22" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bfcf71a-fd64-5c92-96e0-a4da19acec22" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-dd9480d9-9bac-5230-b263-f51496c63b37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd9480d9-9bac-5230-b263-f51496c63b37" "answer-43c39ed1-3288-5a6c-931d-fabc4fa8ff45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd9480d9-9bac-5230-b263-f51496c63b37" :answer/id #uuid "dd9480d9-9bac-5230-b263-f51496c63b37" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "dependent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43c39ed1-3288-5a6c-931d-fabc4fa8ff45" :answer/id #uuid "43c39ed1-3288-5a6c-931d-fabc4fa8ff45" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "independent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152610 :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{2x}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{e}^{-2x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-2{e}^{-2x})-{e}^{-2x}(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186150167 :answer-field/correct 17592186150169 :answer-field/choices ["answer-53649d44-816e-5797-bda4-28adc599b7c3" "answer-9e5866a6-934d-5826-8186-efe3dbb229ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53649d44-816e-5797-bda4-28adc599b7c3" :answer/id #uuid "53649d44-816e-5797-bda4-28adc599b7c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e5866a6-934d-5826-8186-efe3dbb229ee" :answer/id #uuid "9e5866a6-934d-5826-8186-efe3dbb229ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-16b925fc-3e3d-5f10-942e-39dcf0cbf5aa" :question/id #uuid "16b925fc-3e3d-5f10-942e-39dcf0cbf5aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-129803" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({e}^{x}){(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16ff275d-27d3-5143-9da4-76df4f6e09e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16ff275d-27d3-5143-9da4-76df4f6e09e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "16ff275d-27d3-5143-9da4-76df4f6e09e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-69383b24-f13a-51a1-ab0a-dc656419036c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5fd0d2ab-5b95-54c5-81e4-e970978944e7" "answer-69383b24-f13a-51a1-ab0a-dc656419036c" "answer-c2e694a4-9850-5329-8699-d8c145b68e2f" "answer-7fc287fc-d96a-5e8a-b37e-db12d52fb251" "answer-2348e74d-3666-5c00-ab7c-13b1cc040188"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fd0d2ab-5b95-54c5-81e4-e970978944e7" :answer/id #uuid "5fd0d2ab-5b95-54c5-81e4-e970978944e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69383b24-f13a-51a1-ab0a-dc656419036c" :answer/id #uuid "69383b24-f13a-51a1-ab0a-dc656419036c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2e694a4-9850-5329-8699-d8c145b68e2f" :answer/id #uuid "c2e694a4-9850-5329-8699-d8c145b68e2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fc287fc-d96a-5e8a-b37e-db12d52fb251" :answer/id #uuid "7fc287fc-d96a-5e8a-b37e-db12d52fb251" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2348e74d-3666-5c00-ab7c-13b1cc040188" :answer/id #uuid "2348e74d-3666-5c00-ab7c-13b1cc040188" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3b78d46d-4b29-5944-9afd-75dc247769fb" :question/id #uuid "3b78d46d-4b29-5944-9afd-75dc247769fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334119" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=-3x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3x & x+1 \\\\ (-3x)^{′} & (x+1)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3x & x+1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3x)(1)-(x+1)(-3) \\\\  & =-3x+3(x+1) \\\\  & =-3x+3x+3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2e342e39-3482-5d77-a23d-de51cf8fa0e1" "answer-field-51f38bb7-a295-503f-99c8-be2e7ecf6bfb" "answer-field-f3a2b6ff-740b-5c73-94e2-69f51c63499e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2e342e39-3482-5d77-a23d-de51cf8fa0e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e342e39-3482-5d77-a23d-de51cf8fa0e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-85a26b10-da99-59e3-809f-f023dad31c45" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c850c71d-7d08-5a39-bf9b-f0c43f99ce6e" "answer-546a4ff5-ae08-5b2d-af1e-53c8fe140e7d" "answer-85a26b10-da99-59e3-809f-f023dad31c45" "answer-85790f7f-c60c-58be-9db4-3349cd6ff648"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c850c71d-7d08-5a39-bf9b-f0c43f99ce6e" :answer/id #uuid "c850c71d-7d08-5a39-bf9b-f0c43f99ce6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-546a4ff5-ae08-5b2d-af1e-53c8fe140e7d" :answer/id #uuid "546a4ff5-ae08-5b2d-af1e-53c8fe140e7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85a26b10-da99-59e3-809f-f023dad31c45" :answer/id #uuid "85a26b10-da99-59e3-809f-f023dad31c45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85790f7f-c60c-58be-9db4-3349cd6ff648" :answer/id #uuid "85790f7f-c60c-58be-9db4-3349cd6ff648" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51f38bb7-a295-503f-99c8-be2e7ecf6bfb" :answer-field/id #uuid "51f38bb7-a295-503f-99c8-be2e7ecf6bfb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-1b2481cb-a2dd-599c-b082-f5aa8177b9b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1b2481cb-a2dd-599c-b082-f5aa8177b9b7" "answer-1a517c0a-31b2-510c-8c55-64c0349a20c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b2481cb-a2dd-599c-b082-f5aa8177b9b7" :answer/id #uuid "1b2481cb-a2dd-599c-b082-f5aa8177b9b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a517c0a-31b2-510c-8c55-64c0349a20c8" :answer/id #uuid "1a517c0a-31b2-510c-8c55-64c0349a20c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3a2b6ff-740b-5c73-94e2-69f51c63499e" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a2b6ff-740b-5c73-94e2-69f51c63499e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-7b5e65e4-0589-5686-9096-d9440bea556a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-600b1bd0-4a54-55ee-8f3d-ea639c33d64a" "answer-7b5e65e4-0589-5686-9096-d9440bea556a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-600b1bd0-4a54-55ee-8f3d-ea639c33d64a" :answer/id #uuid "600b1bd0-4a54-55ee-8f3d-ea639c33d64a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "dependent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b5e65e4-0589-5686-9096-d9440bea556a" :answer/id #uuid "7b5e65e4-0589-5686-9096-d9440bea556a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "independent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308562 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308124 :answer-field/correct 17592186306652 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307992 :answer-field/correct 17592186306501 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-46d6b627-57fc-5c4f-9c8a-dc31a7241e7a" :question/id #uuid "46d6b627-57fc-5c4f-9c8a-dc31a7241e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334252" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2)-x(2x)+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f206bfdf-3b37-567e-9742-b62602b37fa9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f206bfdf-3b37-567e-9742-b62602b37fa9" :answer-field/id #uuid "f206bfdf-3b37-567e-9742-b62602b37fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c81d1e22-0260-5ad3-9abd-8fe77f612e71" "answer-80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" "answer-8096790f-b2a3-5ac1-9707-fe01377b65c5" "answer-4526afa8-bdbb-5355-a82f-899c0edd5093" "answer-40772f11-35fc-5f4f-bda3-7d228823b749"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c81d1e22-0260-5ad3-9abd-8fe77f612e71" :answer/id #uuid "c81d1e22-0260-5ad3-9abd-8fe77f612e71" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" :answer/id #uuid "80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8096790f-b2a3-5ac1-9707-fe01377b65c5" :answer/id #uuid "8096790f-b2a3-5ac1-9707-fe01377b65c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4526afa8-bdbb-5355-a82f-899c0edd5093" :answer/id #uuid "4526afa8-bdbb-5355-a82f-899c0edd5093" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40772f11-35fc-5f4f-bda3-7d228823b749" :answer/id #uuid "40772f11-35fc-5f4f-bda3-7d228823b749" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153330 :tutorial/content "Suppose $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$ is a set of functions defined on an interval $I\\subseteq R.$\n\n- The set is called **linearly dependent** on $I$ if there exist real numbers $k_1, k_2, \\ldots, k_n,$ *not all zero*, such that\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$  \n\nIntuitively, at least one of the functions can be written as a linear combination of the others.\n\n- The set is called **linearly independent** on $I$ if the *only* way the linear combination\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x)\n$$\n\ncan equal $0$ for all $x \\in I$ is by taking\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions closely parallel the notions of linear dependence and independence for vectors in a vector space.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set of functions \n\n$$\n\\{ \\sin x, \\, \\cos x, \\, 2\\sin x \\}\n$$\n\ndefined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\sin x +\n0 \\cdot \\cos x +\n(-1) \\cdot 2\\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since not all the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly dependent.\n\nLet's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153328 :tutorial/content "Suppose $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are differentiable functions defined on some interval $I.$ Then, the **Wronskian** is defined as \n![](images/57/57c395afa6eecd7289b91a25e61b5310447cc3702daf019b0046615242f34057.svg)\nand it has the following important properties:\n\nIf $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\nIf $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot deduce any information from $W = 0$ in cases where $y_1$ and $y_2$ are not known to be solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153329 :tutorial/content "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of degree $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are the solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{2x})}^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of degree two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152305 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
